        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Bauhaus</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> (1919–1925), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> (1925–1932) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> (1932–1933); afterwards carried by emigrants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> (Chicago, Harvard) and the White City of Tel Aviv</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, 1919–1925, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a>, with Johannes Itten’s preliminary course to 1923; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, 1925–1932, under Gropius to 1928, Hannes Meyer 1928–1930 and Ludwig Mies van der Rohe from 1930; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, 1932–1933, closed by its own leadership in 1933 under pressure from the Nazi regime</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a>, founder and director 1919–1928; Hannes Meyer, director 1928–1930, a radical functionalist; Ludwig Mies van der Rohe, director 1930–1933; teachers Johannes Itten, Lyonel Feininger, Gerhard Marcks, Oskar Schlemmer, Paul Klee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a>, Gunta Stölzl and László Moholy-Nagy; Marcel Breuer, designer of the Wassily Chair</span></div>
