    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Bauhaus</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-gropius.jpg" alt="Walter Gropius" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Walter Gropius <span class="kid-tale-cr">Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 100 years ago, Walter Gropius started an art school in Germany called the Bauhaus.</p><p>He wanted to bring all the arts together and to train artists to work with industry, the makers of goods.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bauhaus.jpg" alt="Bauhaus" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Bauhaus <span class="kid-tale-cr">Spyrosdrakopoulos, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Bauhaus designs had no decoration, just simple shapes, rounded corners and long rows of windows.</p><p>The Bauhaus product that earned the most money was its wallpaper.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky.jpg" alt="Wassily Kandinsky" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Wassily Kandinsky <span class="kid-tale-cr">Vassily Kandinsky by Adolf Elnain Photo credits : Georges Meguerditchian - Centre Pompidou, MNAM-CCI /Dist. RMN-GP Im..., Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Famous painters taught there, such as Paul Klee and Wassily Kandinsky.</p><p>First-year students learned about design and colour and tried out different materials.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weimar.jpg" alt="Weimar" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Weimar <span class="kid-tale-cr">Thuringius, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The school had to move from Weimar to Dessau, and then to Berlin.</p><p>The Nazis called it “un-German”, and under their pressure the school closed itself.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dessau.jpg" alt="Dessau" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Dessau <span class="kid-tale-cr">Sven Scharr, CC BY 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Its teachers carried its ideas to Britain, the United States and Tel Aviv.</p><p>In Dessau, apartment buildings it built are still in use, and several Bauhaus sites are World Heritage sites.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a> founded the Bauhaus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1919 to bring all the arts together and to train artists to work with industry.</li><li>Driven out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1925 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1932, the school was closed by its own leadership in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1933 under pressure from the Nazis, whose writers had labelled it “un-German”.</li><li>Its émigré teachers carried its ideas to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Tel Aviv, and its preliminary course became a common feature of design schools in many countries.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The most profitable product of the Bauhaus, a school remembered for its chairs and buildings, was its wallpaper.</p></aside>
    </section>

